[FINAL-TEXT] The evidence is conclusive. I now have everything needed for the final diagnosis.

**Summary of findings:**

| Device | Key Evidence |
|---|---|
| `router_core_1` | Running config is **entirely empty** — no `router ospf` block, no `network` statements. OSPF is not configured at all. |
| `switch_dist_1_1` | Routing table has **only 2 directly-connected routes** (10.1.1.0/24, 172.16.0.6/31). No OSPF-learned routes, no path to 10.200.0.0/24 or any remote subnet. |
| `router_core_2` eth0 | Points at router_core_1 — **0 neighbors / 0 adj** (expected peer never sends OSPF hellos) |
| `router_core_3` eth0 | Points at router_core_1 — **0 neighbors / 0 adj** |
| `switch_dist_2_1` (healthy peer) | 8 OSPF routes, full adjacency via `router_core_2` — confirms the fault is isolated to the `router_core_1` branch |

The networking team's "physical cable" theory is **refuted** — all interfaces are administratively and physically UP; the ICMP "Destination Net Unreachable" was generated by `switch_dist_1_1` because it has no route, not because of a link failure.

